# GitHub Actions Windows Release

Build Windows NSIS installer and create GitHub Release on push to `main`.

## When to Use

- After building a Windows NSIS installer via `npm run package -- --win nsis --x64 --publish never`
- On every push to `main` branch (automatic release creation)
- When user requests Windows release to appear in GitHub Releases
- Before staging deployment — verify release artifacts are present

## Workflow Structure

The workflow `.github/workflows/windows-build.yml` should include:

### 1. Permissions

Add `permissions: contents: write` at the job level. This is required for the
GitHub Actions token to create releases. Without this, the `softprops/action-gh-release@v2`
action will fail with 403 "Resource not accessible by integration".

```yaml
jobs:
  windows:
    runs-on: windows-latest
    permissions:
      contents: write
      id-token: write
```

### 2. Build Step

Build the NSIS installer:

```yaml
- name: Build Windows NSIS installer
  run: npm run package -- --win nsis --x64 --publish never
```

### 3. Create GitHub Release

Use `softprops/action-gh-release@v2` to create a release with the built artifacts.
Only runs on `main` branch pushes:

```yaml
- name: Create GitHub Release
  if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
  uses: softprops/action-gh-release@v2
  with:
    tag_name: v${{ github.run_number }}
    name: Applyer Indonesia v${{ github.run_number }}
    body: |
      Release for Applyer Indonesia
      Built on ${{ github.run_date }}

      This release contains the Windows NSIS installer for Applyer Indonesia.
    files: |
      release/*.exe
      release/*.blockmap
      release/latest*.yml
    draft: false
    prerelease: false
    overwrite_files: true
```

## Pitfalls

- **403 error on release creation**: Always add `permissions: contents: write` at the
  job level. The GitHub Actions default token lacks this scope.

- **Release already exists**: Set `overwrite_files: true` to allow re-creating the
  same tag. Without this, the workflow will fail if a release with that tag already
  exists.

- **Windows path issues**: Ensure `release/` directory contains the expected files
  (`*.exe`, `*.blockmap`, `latest*.yml`) before the release step. The build step
  must complete successfully first.

- **Node.js deprecation warnings**: The workflow runs on Node 24 but the project
  may have Node 20 deprecation notices. These are cosmetic and don't affect the
  build or release.

## Related Checks

### Preflight Validation

Run `ci-preflight-validation` skill before pushing to confirm:

1. Migration version uniqueness (if SQL migrations exist)
2. Go build succeeds (`go build ./...`)
3. TypeScript typecheck passes (`npm run typecheck`)
4. Lint passes (`npm run lint`)

### Post-Push Verification

After push, verify the release was created:

```bash
# List recent runs
gh run list --limit 5

# View a specific run
gh run view <run_id> --log

# Check the release URL
open https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/releases/tag/v<run_number>
```

## 2026 Toolchain Standard

- Node.js: CI uses Node 24, Dockerfiles must match
- Go: Use go 1.25.12 baseline across all services
- Migration timestamps: YYYYMMDDHHmmSS with current year prefix
- OWASP Top 2025 is the active 2026 standard

